WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.571
♪ ♪

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:11.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Lost civilizations.
 Mysterious sunken cities.</i>

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:16.416
<i> And legendary Atlantis itself.</i>

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:24.390
<i> Vanished beneath the waves.</i>

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:28.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
PATRICK: There is a
huge amount of unrecorded
human history beneath the

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:32.766
ocean's surface that we don't
know very much about at all.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Imagine if we
 could empty the oceans,
 letting the water drain away</i>

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:44.076 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> to reveal the secrets
 of the sea floor.</i>

00:00:45.044 --> 00:00:49.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Now we can, using the latest
 underwater technology.</i>

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:58.291 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Piercing the deep oceans
 and turning accurate
 data into 3D images.</i>

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:07.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Is this a huge
 underwater temple carved by a
 mysterious lost civilization?</i>

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.910
<i> NARRATOR: Why are these
 perfectly circular stones</i>

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.781
<i> lying at the bottom of
 a bay in the Aegean Sea?</i>

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:26.552
SIMON: There are shapes
on the seabed that
just don't make sense.

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:31.224 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Are these just
 rocks, or the remains
 of an ancient city?</i>

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:34.994 align:start position:0%
<i>JON (over radio): Parts of
this are just beginning to
be revealed for the first time.</i>

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.462
<i> Wow.</i>

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.902 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: And is the real
 site of Atlantis finally
 about to be revealed?</i>

00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:45.037
-People want to believe
in places like Atlantis.

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:47.540 align:start size:81% position:19%
They want to believe
in other worlds.

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:05.258
(waves crashing)

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:15.535 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Stories
 of sunken cities have
 fascinated for millennia.</i>

00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:22.442
<i> The most tantalizing
 of all: Atlantis.</i>

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:39.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> First described by
 ancient philosopher,
 Plato, a dazzling civilization</i>

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:45.064 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> destroyed by the gods as
 punishment for human pride.</i>

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:59.679 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
-People connect to that story,
and it's because of this idea
of an ancient civilization,

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:03.949 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
a utopia, that
we've evolved from.

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:07.386 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: 2,000 years
 later, the idea of Atlantis</i>

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:11.323
<i> continues to fascinate
 A-list archaeologists.</i>

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:15.428
<i> And the producers
 of B-list movies.</i>

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.497
<i> MAN (over TV): Atlantis is the
story of those who, like today,</i>

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:22.168 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> would use the
 marvels of science to
 conquer and enslave.</i>

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:25.171
<i> Atlantis, a world
 that worships strange
 gods of science,</i>

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:28.374 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> of science, a
 science gone berserk.</i>

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:29.741
-Plato would have
loved this okay?

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:31.544 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
He would have
had a great time.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.145 line:0%
He would have said, 'at
last, you know, people
are listening to me.

00:03:33.245 --> 00:03:34.647 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
People are taking
my story seriously.'

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.950 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And he would have
enjoyed this immensely.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Using science,
 not science fiction,
 draining the oceans</i>

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:45.124 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> exposes new evidence
 about Atlantis.</i>

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:49.995
<i> And other stories of
 great sunken cities,
 around the world.</i>

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:56.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> As the waters recede
 from around the remote
 Japanese island of Yonaguni,</i>

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:01.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> a mysterious formation
 begins to appear.</i>

00:04:08.947 --> 00:04:12.952
<i> Could it be the creation of
 an advanced, ancient people?</i>

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.493 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> Yonaguni lies in
 the East China Sea.</i>

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.200
<i> The waters here are perilous.</i>

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:34.474 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Schools of hammerhead
 sharks patrol, amid
 powerful currents.</i>

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:40.980
<i> Typhoons frequently
 batter the island.</i>

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:48.554
<i> For divers, it's the
 ultimate challenge.</i>

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.495
<i> Kihachiro Aratake is one of
 the pioneers of diving here.</i>

00:04:56.662 --> 00:05:01.801 line:0%
KIHACHIRO: The water
was crystal clear.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:08.908
I felt as though I was
looking down from the
seabed from the sky.

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:12.645
<i> NARRATOR: Searching for
 a new dive site, he makes
 a startling discovery.</i>

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:27.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
-I saw a steps-like formation,
like terraced fields.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:35.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
I thought for a moment
it was similar to the
Machu Picchu ruins.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.377
So, I named the spot
the submarine ruins.

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:50.083 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: It's an
 extraordinary spectacle.</i>

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:58.024
<i> The size of five
 football fields.</i>

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:08.401
<i> Smooth-sided walls rise up
 to the summit, the height
 of an eight-story building.</i>

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:17.009
<i> Flights of stone steps
 climb up from the base.</i>

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:23.049
<i> And on every side, the
 shapes and forms are
 strikingly regular.</i>

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
-It can't be formed naturally,
without human involvement.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.026
It couldn't be like that.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.663 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> NARRATOR: So,
 what could it be?</i>

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:45.071
<i> It's impossible to investigate
 the structure fully by diving.</i>

00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:56.282 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The only way is to conduct
 a survey using the latest
 scanning technology.</i>

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:05.991 line:0%
HIRONOBU: Although the shallow
waters around Yonaguni Island
are close to human habitation,

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:09.695 line:0%
we don't know much about them.

00:07:09.961 --> 00:07:11.831
It's an unknown frontier.

00:07:18.937 --> 00:07:22.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Using sonar scans,
 Hironobu Kan records a</i>

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:25.611
<i> complete digital map
 of the sea floor.</i>

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:32.451 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Using this data,
 it's possible, for
 the very first time,</i>

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:38.891 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> to drain away the waters
 and reveal what lies
 beneath in exact detail.</i>

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:47.667
<i> The summit of the
 mysterious structure
 begins to appear.</i>

00:07:56.041 --> 00:07:58.110
(waves crashing)

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:03.816
<i> Water pours off its smooth
 terraced layers, exposing
 them once again to the sky.</i>

00:08:09.321 --> 00:08:14.660 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And with the monument
 left high and dry, its
 true scale becomes clear.</i>

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:20.399
<i> Vast, imposing
 and spectacular.</i>

00:08:26.805 --> 00:08:30.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The incredible discovery
 attracts worldwide attention.</i>

00:08:32.244 --> 00:08:37.350
<i> Divers, journalists
 and TV crews flock to
 Yonaguni, all asking:</i>

00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:41.254 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> what could this
 mysterious structure be?</i>

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.691
<i> And scientists come here too.</i>

00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:50.329 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Among them is Professor
 Masaaki Kimura, one of
 Japan's top marine geologists.</i>

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:59.105 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
KIMURA: When I dived
underwater to explore it I
felt right away that it must

00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:01.073
be a man-made structure.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:09.015
<i> NARRATOR: He finds several
 features that he believes
 indicate human activity.</i>

00:09:17.789 --> 00:09:21.994 align:start size:91% position:9%
-Here, you'll see
something that looks like
the front gate of a castle.

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:24.930 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like a tunnel,
and you go through it.

00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:30.436
<i> NARRATOR: On the far side
 of the tunnel, a road loops
 around to a flight of steps.</i>

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:35.174 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Watched over by two
 mysterious stone objects.</i>

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:45.150 align:start size:88% position:13%
-There are mound-like
bulges here and here,
and if you look at them,

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:50.089
you'll find turtles with their
necks extended, on both sides.

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:55.527
<i> NARRATOR: According to
 ancient Japanese folklore,
 the dragon god, Ryujin,</i>

00:09:55.627 --> 00:10:00.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> lives in an underwater palace,
 watched over by turtles.</i>

00:10:00.799 --> 00:10:04.136
<i> To Professor Kimura, this
 indicates that the monument</i>

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:07.573
<i> is so important
 it needs guarding.</i>

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.276
<i> And that's not all.</i>

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:17.316
-If you go up this path
you get to this place.

00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:19.418
This portion is triangular.

00:10:19.518 --> 00:10:23.022 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's why it's called
the triangle pool.

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:27.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
Since this is fairly
big and shallow as well,
you can relax in it.

00:10:28.493 --> 00:10:34.132
Also, the sea surface is
very close to it and it's
warm due to the sunlight.

00:10:34.232 --> 00:10:38.270
You feel chilly after
you come out of the
deeper area down there,

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.673
and you can warm up here.

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:47.579
<i> NARRATOR: But the monument's
 most striking feature
 is its wide, flat terraces,</i>

00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:51.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Large enough to support
 thousands of people.</i>

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:56.955 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Professor Kimura believes
 the gateway, stairs,
 turtles and terraces</i>

00:10:57.056 --> 00:11:00.026
<i> all point towards one purpose.</i>

00:11:09.535 --> 00:11:13.706 align:start size:81% position:19%
-Judging from its
shape, I think they
used it as a castle.

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:20.179 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: But to prove his
 theory, Professor Kimura
 needs more:</i>

00:11:20.279 --> 00:11:24.149
<i> the artifacts of those
 who once lived here.</i>

00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:28.153
<i> He scours the structure
 looking for clues, and</i>

00:11:28.253 --> 00:11:32.157
<i> discovers what look
 like primitive tools.</i>

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:38.631
<i> Then, hidden behind one of
 the great walls, the most
 remarkable find of all.</i>

00:11:42.334 --> 00:11:46.371 align:start size:84% position:16%
-Look at this,
a symbol is inscribed
here and here's a hole.

00:11:46.472 --> 00:11:49.141 align:start size:84% position:16%
Probably, they hung
this article like this.

00:11:49.241 --> 00:11:51.276
And here's something
looking like a letter.

00:11:51.376 --> 00:11:55.214 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe it could be a kind
of a talisman to protect
people from evil.

00:11:55.681 --> 00:11:58.350 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: For Professor
 Kimura, the case is closed.</i>

00:11:58.451 --> 00:12:03.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The artifacts and
 the monument's regular shapes
 prove that humans lived here,</i>

00:12:03.822 --> 00:12:06.057
<i> 8,000 years ago.</i>

00:12:06.157 --> 00:12:12.398
<i> After that, rising sea levels
 claim the territory of this
 lost race of master builders.</i>

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:15.468
<i> But is that the real
 story of Yonaguni?</i>

00:12:23.108 --> 00:12:26.912 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Professor
 Kimura believes that this
 extraordinary structure</i>

00:12:27.012 --> 00:12:32.051
<i> off the coast of Japan is a
 gigantic castle, built long
 before the pyramids of Egypt</i>

00:12:33.818 --> 00:12:37.055
<i> by a mysterious
 lost civilization.</i>

00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:39.759
<i> But is it?</i>

00:12:43.929 --> 00:12:50.302
<i>With the water drained away,
it's possible to scrutinize the
monument in forensic detail.</i>

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:59.345
<i> The edges appear regular and
 straight, as if made from
 blocks carved by human hand.</i>

00:13:01.913 --> 00:13:05.784
<i> But closer examination
 reveals something else.</i>

00:13:06.284 --> 00:13:10.556 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> The surfaces are
 smooth and unbroken.</i>

00:13:10.656 --> 00:13:14.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The monument is not assembled
 from handmade blocks,</i>

00:13:14.526 --> 00:13:19.632
<i> but is in fact one
 solid mass of rock.</i>

00:13:24.236 --> 00:13:28.907 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
HIRONOBU: The pyramid area
and the headland behind it
are connected with each other.

00:13:29.007 --> 00:13:33.745
The surface of the terrain
has a lot of grooves, so you
may think they are separate,

00:13:33.845 --> 00:13:38.984
but since they consist of
the identical stone, they
are originally connected.

00:13:41.319 --> 00:13:45.423
<i> NARRATOR: The basic
 form of the monument
 is clearly natural.</i>

00:13:46.591 --> 00:13:51.630 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> But what about the gateway,
 steps, turtles and pool?</i>

00:13:53.766 --> 00:13:57.936
<i> Could it be that an
 ancient people carved
 into this huge rock,</i>

00:13:58.036 --> 00:14:02.374
<i> transforming it into a
 great castle or temple?</i>

00:14:04.709 --> 00:14:08.813
<i> Studying the headland on
 shore provides the answer.</i>

00:14:10.114 --> 00:14:14.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The shapes here are
 strikingly similar to
 those found on the monument,</i>

00:14:14.419 --> 00:14:19.057 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> and that's because
 they were formed in
 exactly the same way.</i>

00:14:19.591 --> 00:14:23.729 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Both are made of
 sandstone which, when
 subjected to stress,</i>

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:28.033 align:start position:0%
<i>such as during
an earthquake, can
fracture along vertical faults,</i>

00:14:28.133 --> 00:14:32.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> forming angular shapes
 and what look like steps.</i>

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:37.008 line:0%
PATRICK: Put something like
that underwater, have the
water wash over the top of it

00:14:37.108 --> 00:14:42.647 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
and clear away all the debris,
and you've got very, very
fine step-like structures.

00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:46.985 align:start size:88% position:13%
But they're no more
human made than any other
structure down there.

00:14:48.053 --> 00:14:51.423 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: The lack of tool
 marks is further confirmation.</i>

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:56.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The forces which
 shaped this remarkable
 place are geological.</i>

00:14:56.862 --> 00:14:58.897
<i> But what of the artifacts?</i>

00:14:58.997 --> 00:15:05.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Perhaps the monument,
 if not built by ancient
 people, was inhabited by them.</i>

00:15:05.137 --> 00:15:07.505
<i> Walking these steps.</i>

00:15:07.605 --> 00:15:11.109 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> Crowding these
 natural terraces.</i>

00:15:12.944 --> 00:15:17.783
<i> But so far, no other objects
 have been found, suggesting
 that the talisman and tools</i>

00:15:19.218 --> 00:15:24.823 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> were dropped from a boat
 passing overhead, landing on
 the monument simply by chance.</i>

00:15:26.658 --> 00:15:29.661 line:0%
-The clear thing for Yonaguni
for me is there's no pottery,

00:15:29.761 --> 00:15:31.730 line:0%
there's no evidence of
actual human occupation.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:34.232 line:0%
There's not a single
wall from the site.

00:15:34.332 --> 00:15:38.303
There's nothing on it, that
indicates human activity.

00:15:39.704 --> 00:15:43.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The Yonaguni
 Monument is an extraordinary
 natural formation,</i>

00:15:43.908 --> 00:15:46.945
<i> created by epic
 geological forces.</i>

00:15:47.846 --> 00:15:51.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But its shapes
 appear so regular
 that many still believe</i>

00:15:51.883 --> 00:15:55.486
<i> it holds a secret,
 ancient purpose.</i>

00:15:56.221 --> 00:16:00.659 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
JIM: You can take
that leap if you have an
imagination and say yes,

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:05.563 line:0%
this could be an ancient
city beneath the sea but
in the case of Yonaguni

00:16:05.663 --> 00:16:08.633 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
I'm in those ranks
that feel that it's not.

00:16:08.733 --> 00:16:10.301
It's geology.

00:16:10.402 --> 00:16:12.904
-We want to believe
that science doesn't
have all the answers.

00:16:13.004 --> 00:16:18.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
We want to believe innately
that there is something out
there that we can connect with

00:16:20.078 --> 00:16:22.714 align:start size:91% position:9%
spiritually and that hasn't
really been polluted by

00:16:22.814 --> 00:16:25.918
being the subject of
scientific discovery.

00:16:27.553 --> 00:16:31.590
<i> NARRATOR: And still
 amongst the believers
 is Professor Kimura.</i>

00:16:32.991 --> 00:16:39.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> He remains convinced that the
 monument was inhabited by his
 ancestors and continues to</i>

00:16:39.164 --> 00:16:43.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> search for evidence to
 prove the doubters wrong.</i>

00:16:49.174 --> 00:16:51.943
<i> Beneath the oceans of
 the world lie many more</i>

00:16:52.043 --> 00:16:56.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> tantalizing traces of
 possible lost civilizations.</i>

00:16:57.716 --> 00:17:02.520
<i> As the water continues
 to drain away, in the
 Eastern Mediterranean,</i>

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:07.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> astonishing structures emerge
 in a quiet island harbor.</i>

00:17:10.595 --> 00:17:15.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Is this jumble of shaped
 stones the architectural
 debris of a once great city?</i>

00:17:23.742 --> 00:17:29.481
<i> Alikanas Bay, a tourist
 hotspot on the island
 of Zakynthos, Greece.</i>

00:17:32.451 --> 00:17:37.589 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Diver, Pavlos Voutos,
 sets out to take some
 underwater photos.</i>

00:17:54.239 --> 00:17:58.710 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Pavlos swims
 farther out into the bay
 in search of clearer water.</i>

00:18:03.148 --> 00:18:07.786
<i> Then, out of the gloom,
 he sees something that
 will change his life.</i>

00:18:33.979 --> 00:18:38.450 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: The debris
 stretches out for hundreds
 of yards in all directions.</i>

00:18:39.851 --> 00:18:45.691 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The area is so large that
 Pavlos is convinced he's found
 the remains of an entire town.</i>

00:19:26.865 --> 00:19:31.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The discovery sends
 a bolt of electricity through
 the world of archaeology.</i>

00:19:34.172 --> 00:19:39.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Professor Michael Stamatakis,
 immediately travels to
 Zakynthos to investigate.</i>

00:19:57.829 --> 00:20:01.466
<i> NARRATOR: Stamatakis recalls
 seeing similar shapes on land,</i>

00:20:01.566 --> 00:20:06.171 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> at the site of an
 ancient settlement built
 over 2,000 years ago.</i>

00:20:24.922 --> 00:20:29.327
<i> NARRATOR: If the same shapes
 lie on the sea bed, they could
 indicate an ancient settlement</i>

00:20:29.427 --> 00:20:34.132 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> just offshore concealed
 under the bay and forgotten
 about for centuries.</i>

00:20:46.043 --> 00:20:49.214 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Comparing the images
 is not enough.</i>

00:20:54.519 --> 00:20:58.356 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The only way to
 reveal a complete
 picture of the structures</i>

00:20:58.456 --> 00:21:02.894 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> is a detailed
 underwater survey.</i>

00:21:02.994 --> 00:21:06.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Simon Brown is an
 expert in 3D modelling.</i>

00:21:06.932 --> 00:21:10.201 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> But the task facing
 him is immense.</i>

00:21:10.802 --> 00:21:13.271 align:start size:81% position:19%
SIMON: Right now
we're not quite sure
how big the area is.

00:21:13.371 --> 00:21:17.808 line:0%
I've estimated it's about
16 acres, which will be

00:21:17.909 --> 00:21:22.481 line:0%
more than double the
largest subject I've
ever covered to date.

00:21:28.653 --> 00:21:30.154
It's a weird place.

00:21:30.721 --> 00:21:35.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
There is definitely features
here that I have never
seen anywhere else before.

00:21:37.695 --> 00:21:39.097
They look out of place.

00:21:44.936 --> 00:21:49.975
But then I started to see
more regular shapes that
looked that cut stone.

00:21:50.075 --> 00:21:54.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
So my mind then starts
to think, is it...

00:21:54.178 --> 00:21:56.114
is it manmade?

00:21:57.983 --> 00:22:01.987 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Can draining the
 waters of the Mediterranean
 provide the answer?</i>

00:22:09.761 --> 00:22:11.996 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Simon Brown
 is mapping mysterious</i>

00:22:12.096 --> 00:22:15.333 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> underwater structures
 discovered in a Greek bay.</i>

00:22:16.167 --> 00:22:20.038 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Could they be the remains
 of an ancient town?</i>

00:22:21.206 --> 00:22:27.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> He takes nearly 4,000 high
 resolution photographs of the
 sea floor tracking each with</i>

00:22:28.814 --> 00:22:32.084
<i> pinpoint accuracy through GPS.</i>

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:40.492
<i> Using these images, it's now
 possible to do something which
 has never been done before:</i>

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:47.165 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> drain the waters of
 Alikanas Bay and reveal,
 for the first time ever,</i>

00:22:47.265 --> 00:22:50.168
<i> a 3D plan of the
 entire sea floor.</i>

00:22:54.639 --> 00:22:58.844
<i> As the Mediterranean begins
 to recede, a world is exposed</i>

00:22:58.944 --> 00:23:02.314
<i> that's been invisible
 for thousands of years.</i>

00:23:04.249 --> 00:23:08.653 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> First, the rocky shoreline
 is left high and dry.</i>

00:23:09.754 --> 00:23:15.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Then, from the
 dark depths, regular
 shapes begin to appear,</i>

00:23:15.293 --> 00:23:17.162
<i> hidden amongst the rocks.</i>

00:23:20.565 --> 00:23:26.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Could they be the bases of
 stone columns which together
 once formed a grand colonnade?</i>

00:23:30.074 --> 00:23:35.146 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The use of colonnades is
 a turning point in ancient
 Greek architecture.</i>

00:23:36.681 --> 00:23:41.886
<i> Builders can now switch
 from wood to stone, a
 far stronger material,</i>

00:23:41.986 --> 00:23:45.090
<i> to create ever larger
 temples to the gods.</i>

00:23:46.725 --> 00:23:50.528
<i> A colonnade in Alikanas Bay
 would prove that an important,</i>

00:23:50.628 --> 00:23:53.331
<i> ancient settlement
 once stood here.</i>

00:23:53.731 --> 00:23:56.768
<i> But some experts
 remain skeptical.</i>

00:23:57.402 --> 00:23:59.771 line:0%
PATRICK: There are structures
all over the world that mimic

00:23:59.871 --> 00:24:01.572 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
something that humans
may have created.

00:24:01.672 --> 00:24:04.308 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
Doesn't mean that
humans created them.

00:24:05.176 --> 00:24:08.846
<i> NARRATOR: With the water
 drained away, it's now
 possible to search the</i>

00:24:08.947 --> 00:24:12.717
<i> sea bed for evidence
 of human occupation.</i>

00:24:13.384 --> 00:24:16.188
<i> Fragments of the pots
 people cooked with.</i>

00:24:17.789 --> 00:24:20.124
<i> Charcoal from their fires.</i>

00:24:20.224 --> 00:24:24.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Tools for farming, and weapons
 for defending their homes.</i>

00:24:25.029 --> 00:24:29.401
<i> Any objects made of
 metal, clay or stone
 should have survived.</i>

00:24:33.872 --> 00:24:36.674
<i> But there's nothing.</i>

00:24:37.242 --> 00:24:41.579 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Which means
 these extraordinary
 remains, whatever they are,</i>

00:24:41.679 --> 00:24:45.116
<i> are not the relics
 of a lost town.</i>

00:24:46.517 --> 00:24:48.119
<i> So, what are they?</i>

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:23.421 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Further analysis
 reveals the formations
 are what geologists call</i>

00:25:23.521 --> 00:25:25.423
<i> 'concretions'.</i>

00:25:25.523 --> 00:25:31.363 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Solid blocks of
 sedimentary rock formed by
 powerful volcanic activity.</i>

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:37.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Underwater vents
 release methane from
 deep within the Earth.</i>

00:25:37.168 --> 00:25:39.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Combining with
 microbes and sediment,</i>

00:25:39.904 --> 00:25:44.008
<i> the methane causes regular
 circular shapes to form.</i>

00:25:44.108 --> 00:25:47.245 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Far from being a
 relic of ancient Greece,</i>

00:25:47.345 --> 00:25:52.450 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the structures here
 pre-date human activities
 by five million years!</i>

00:25:56.254 --> 00:25:58.990 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Despite all the evidence
 against his theory,</i>

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:02.794 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Pavlos Voutos remains
 convinced that the fantastical</i>

00:26:02.894 --> 00:26:06.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> landscape he discovered
 was shaped by his ancestors.</i>

00:26:26.217 --> 00:26:28.252 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Classical
 civilizations dominate the</i>

00:26:28.353 --> 00:26:31.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Mediterranean for
 over a thousand years.</i>

00:26:31.689 --> 00:26:36.561 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Today, all around
 its coast, people tell
 stories of sunken cities.</i>

00:26:39.831 --> 00:26:43.168 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As the waters around
 Greece drain away further,</i>

00:26:43.268 --> 00:26:47.038 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> an extraordinary underwater
 vista is exposed.</i>

00:26:47.972 --> 00:26:52.877 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Could these patterns on the
 sea floor be the remnants
 of an ancient city?</i>

00:26:58.082 --> 00:27:02.854 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The village of Elafonisos
 sits on a small island
 just off the Greek coast.</i>

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:12.830
<i> At first glance, an
 ordinary fishing port.</i>

00:27:14.132 --> 00:27:19.604
<i> But in 1967, less than 100
 yards out to sea, something
 attracts the attention of a</i>

00:27:21.105 --> 00:27:24.141 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> visiting maritime
 archaeologist.</i>

00:27:24.842 --> 00:27:29.647
<i> Snorkeling in 13 feet
 of water, Nicholas Fleming
 catches a glimpse of</i>

00:27:29.747 --> 00:27:32.384 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> regular shapes
 on the sea floor.</i>

00:27:34.953 --> 00:27:39.924 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Then, exploring deeper,
 he sees what he thinks is
 evidence of human activity.</i>

00:27:41.626 --> 00:27:45.996 line:0%
FLEMING: I looked at these
rows of stones, and I just
had no idea what it was,

00:27:46.096 --> 00:27:50.835 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
but I realized immediately
that it was manmade, that
we were looking at a

00:27:50.935 --> 00:27:55.306
large part of a town, and
I mean I just went crazy.

00:27:56.241 --> 00:28:00.611
<i> NARRATOR: Fleming is
 convinced he has found
 something special.</i>

00:28:00.711 --> 00:28:05.750 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> But could these simply
 be natural formations,
 mimicking human structures,</i>

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:09.086 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> like the concretions
 of nearby Alikanas Bay?</i>

00:28:09.654 --> 00:28:13.758
<i> The following year,
 Fleming returns with
 a team of surveyors</i>

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:16.094
<i> to discover the truth.</i>

00:28:17.261 --> 00:28:21.365
<i> Using tape measures, they
 document what they find.</i>

00:28:21.465 --> 00:28:24.668
<i> And the results
 are astonishing.</i>

00:28:25.603 --> 00:28:29.841
<i> The site is strewn with
 signs of human activity.</i>

00:28:30.241 --> 00:28:36.647 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Pots, storage vessels
 and tools lie across a
 huge area of the sea bed.</i>

00:28:36.981 --> 00:28:42.786 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> There can be no doubt:
 Fleming has discovered the
 remains of an ancient town.</i>

00:28:43.621 --> 00:28:47.625
<i> It's named Pavlopetri
 after a nearby island.</i>

00:28:48.492 --> 00:28:51.029
<i> But who built it, and when?</i>

00:28:53.564 --> 00:28:57.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> These questions inspire a
 generation of archaeologists.</i>

00:28:57.769 --> 00:29:01.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
JON: Well I've always
been interested in submerged
settlement, submerged towns,

00:29:02.006 --> 00:29:03.975
and I'd read about
Pavlopetri as a boy.

00:29:04.075 --> 00:29:09.047 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
And this idea of an intact,
prehistoric town underwater
just seemed you know

00:29:09.147 --> 00:29:11.349
incredibly romantic,
incredibly exciting.

00:29:19.357 --> 00:29:24.528 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Henderson can now
 fulfill a childhood dream
 and explore this haunted</i>

00:29:24.629 --> 00:29:27.231
<i> underwater world for himself.</i>

00:29:27.598 --> 00:29:29.467
-Here we have a quern stone.

00:29:29.567 --> 00:29:33.704 align:start size:84% position:16%
-You can see it's
got a very concave,
concave surface there.

00:29:33.804 --> 00:29:39.210
That's for grinding grain or
whatever it was that you were
turning into you know flour.

00:29:40.811 --> 00:29:42.112
That's quite a clear artifact.

00:29:42.213 --> 00:29:44.482
Very nice.

00:29:50.188 --> 00:29:52.222
There is a huge amphora.

00:29:52.323 --> 00:29:55.994 align:start size:91% position:9%
One, amphora, two amphora,
three, four, five.

00:29:58.329 --> 00:30:00.697
This gives you an example of
what's coming out of the sand,

00:30:00.798 --> 00:30:02.900
what's eroding out of the sand.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.604
Look, there's the base of an
amphora, there's the amphora
shape there, it's on its side.

00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:11.009
There's another one next to
it and another one there.

00:30:14.045 --> 00:30:19.017
<i> NARRATOR: And further into
 the bay, more evidence
 of human life, and death.</i>

00:30:20.451 --> 00:30:25.590 align:start size:88% position:13%
-So, here's a cist grave.
Edge set slabs.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.195 align:start size:81% position:19%
A very small one.
Possibly for a baby.

00:30:31.896 --> 00:30:34.732
There are about 40
of these on the site.

00:30:38.369 --> 00:30:40.538
<i> NARRATOR: Diving gives
 intriguing glimpses of</i>

00:30:40.638 --> 00:30:43.508 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> objects lying
 upon the sea bed.</i>

00:30:44.275 --> 00:30:47.078
<i> But it's impossible to
 gain a complete picture.</i>

00:30:52.049 --> 00:30:56.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> To do this, it must be mapped
 using the latest in underwater
 scanning technology.</i>

00:31:05.796 --> 00:31:08.466
(waves crashing)

00:31:14.538 --> 00:31:20.411
<i> Using this data, it's now
 possible to drain away the
 waters above Pavlopetri and</i>

00:31:21.412 --> 00:31:26.618
<i> bring sunlight back to
 a forgotten world and
 a true Sunken City.</i>

00:31:34.858 --> 00:31:40.164 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> With the entire
 bay drained away,
 everything becomes clear.</i>

00:31:44.435 --> 00:31:49.874 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The lines are the
 foundations of buildings,
 laid out in clear streets.</i>

00:31:51.976 --> 00:31:56.748 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> At the heart of the town is
 a large open area, perhaps
 an ancient market place.</i>

00:32:02.553 --> 00:32:08.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And nearby, the remnants
 of a huge structure, perhaps
 an important civic building.</i>

00:32:11.862 --> 00:32:16.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The remains cover 20
 acres and the town could
 have housed 4,000 people.</i>

00:32:19.336 --> 00:32:23.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> A thriving, ancient
 settlement once existed here.</i>

00:32:24.642 --> 00:32:28.947
<i> The next challenge is to
 discover who built it.</i>

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:31.382
<i> And when.</i>

00:32:33.617 --> 00:32:37.488 align:start size:91% position:9%
-We know how old the
occupation of the site is,
because we've got pottery.

00:32:37.588 --> 00:32:41.225
And pottery particularly
in this part of the
world is very well dated.

00:32:41.926 --> 00:32:44.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
What was really
exciting is we actually found
evicdence of pottery

00:32:44.728 --> 00:32:47.265
from the end of the Stone Age.

00:32:47.598 --> 00:32:51.768
So, we've actually got
occupation here from
about 5,500 years ago.

00:32:51.868 --> 00:32:54.305 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then we've got
every single bit of
pottery you would expect

00:32:54.405 --> 00:32:56.107
up until about 1100 BC.

00:32:56.207 --> 00:32:58.209
There's no gaps in that.

00:32:58.309 --> 00:33:01.412
So, the place seems to have
been continuously occupied
throughout the Bronze Age.

00:33:03.447 --> 00:33:07.751 align:start position:0%
<i>NARRATOR: Pavlopetri was
occupied for 2,400 years during</i>

00:33:07.851 --> 00:33:11.355
<i> a revolutionary period
 of human development.</i>

00:33:12.489 --> 00:33:18.096
<i> At its peak in the bronze age,
 around 1700BC, it looked like
 this: a perfect example of the</i>

00:33:20.965 --> 00:33:26.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> time when Europeans start
 to live in stone-built towns
 with regular laid out streets.</i>

00:33:28.472 --> 00:33:31.275
<i> But one question remains.</i>

00:33:31.609 --> 00:33:36.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Why did the builders of
 this town choose this
 particular location,</i>

00:33:36.113 --> 00:33:38.916
<i> in a remote part
 of ancient Greece?</i>

00:33:41.185 --> 00:33:44.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Exploring the
 drained landscape
 around the ancient city</i>

00:33:44.922 --> 00:33:47.725
<i> reveals an all-important clue.</i>

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:53.897
<i> At its edge, a huge sand bank
 stretches out into the ocean.</i>

00:33:53.997 --> 00:33:58.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> During the Bronze Age,
 people don't build
 ports out of stone.</i>

00:33:58.969 --> 00:34:02.639
<i> They bring ships up to the
 shore and then beach them.</i>

00:34:03.874 --> 00:34:09.380 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
<i> And Pavlopetri's location
 means it can exploit the trade
 routes that boom during the</i>

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:12.583 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
<i> Bronze Age right across
 the Mediterranean.</i>

00:34:15.753 --> 00:34:19.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Evidence of early commercial
 activity is everywhere.</i>

00:34:19.757 --> 00:34:22.926
-The interesting thing is
we've clearly got a lot
of material coming in.

00:34:23.026 --> 00:34:26.296 align:start size:91% position:9%
We found thousands
and thousands of shards of
very large storage vessels,

00:34:26.397 --> 00:34:29.433
which have been used for
storing olive oil, wine,

00:34:29.533 --> 00:34:31.302 align:start size:84% position:16%
that kind of thing and
generally anything.

00:34:31.402 --> 00:34:33.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
They were like the
containers of the day.

00:34:34.272 --> 00:34:35.672
<i> NARRATOR: And that's not all.</i>

00:34:35.773 --> 00:34:40.210
<i> Pavlopetri is also a
 hub for manufactory.</i>

00:34:40.310 --> 00:34:44.615 align:start size:91% position:9%
-We've got a lot of
evidence for loom weights,
thousands of loom weights,

00:34:44.715 --> 00:34:48.218
way more than you would need
for domestic production so it
seems that maybe textiles were

00:34:48.319 --> 00:34:51.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
something that were being
produced on the site
and perhaps exported.

00:34:55.192 --> 00:34:59.030 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Pavlopetri is an
 extraordinary, bustling,
 vibrant port city.</i>

00:35:00.197 --> 00:35:02.800
<i> And an important
 center of trade.</i>

00:35:03.067 --> 00:35:06.404 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> So how did such an advanced
 metropolis meet its end?</i>

00:35:14.945 --> 00:35:19.183 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: An ancient,
 submerged city off
 the coast of Greece.</i>

00:35:19.283 --> 00:35:22.519
<i> How it came to lie on the
 sea floor is a mystery.</i>

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.090 align:start size:91% position:9%
JON: One of the interesting
things about Pavlopetri and
its submergence is that

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:28.425
there are no historical
references to it at all.

00:35:28.525 --> 00:35:31.595
It sunk under the waves
and then it was out
of sight, out of mind.

00:35:31.696 --> 00:35:34.664 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's certainly no
classical sources that
refer to its sinking.

00:35:34.765 --> 00:35:38.269 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's nothing until
it's discovery really
in the 20th century.

00:35:41.405 --> 00:35:46.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Scanning
 the drained bay could reveal
 what happened to Pavlopetri.</i>

00:35:48.245 --> 00:35:52.049
<i> At the edge of the town,
 it's possible to make out
 three mysterious lines.</i>

00:35:55.419 --> 00:36:00.791
<i> These are strips of beach
 rock, formed by the action
 of sunlight on salt water.</i>

00:36:02.092 --> 00:36:04.595 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> They form only
 near the shoreline.</i>

00:36:05.329 --> 00:36:11.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It can mean only one thing:
 earthquakes caused this land
 to subside into the sea.</i>

00:36:12.803 --> 00:36:15.739 align:start size:91% position:9%
-So, there was a big
earthquake, perhaps part of
the remains went under the

00:36:15.839 --> 00:36:19.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
water, part of them are
still surviving, then there
was another earthquake it was

00:36:19.276 --> 00:36:21.946
pushed under then there was
a third, and it was gone.

00:36:24.515 --> 00:36:29.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Pavlopetri is one
 of the most important Bronze
 age sites in the world,</i>

00:36:29.753 --> 00:36:33.191
<i> and the oldest submerged
 town ever discovered.</i>

00:36:35.926 --> 00:36:39.096
<i> But it's not alone.</i>

00:36:48.005 --> 00:36:51.909
-A lot of the major finds in
the next couple of decades are
going to come from the sea.

00:36:52.009 --> 00:36:56.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think we have the
potential to rewrite what
we know about human history.

00:36:58.282 --> 00:37:04.222
<i> NARRATOR: Pavlopetri is an
 extraordinary find, but it is
 not the legendary Atlantis.</i>

00:37:07.124 --> 00:37:11.261
<i> Various locations for
 Plato's dazzling city
 have been proposed,</i>

00:37:11.361 --> 00:37:13.263
<i> around the world.</i>

00:37:13.864 --> 00:37:19.437 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> But most academic
 investigations point to one
 place: the Mediterranean.</i>

00:37:20.938 --> 00:37:24.942 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> As its waters continue
 to drain away,
 at its western end,</i>

00:37:25.042 --> 00:37:28.345
<i> new and tantalizing
 evidence is revealed.</i>

00:37:28.879 --> 00:37:34.986 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Could archaeologists
 finally have found the site
 of Plato's famous lost city?</i>

00:37:40.924 --> 00:37:45.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Marc Gutscher
 is a geophysicist, long
 fascinated by Plato's story.</i>

00:37:45.963 --> 00:37:49.232
MARC: I do think that
becoming interested in
Atlantis can be almost,

00:37:49.333 --> 00:37:53.537 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
like a bug or an infection
or like gambling.

00:37:53.637 --> 00:37:57.073 line:0%
I think one can
become obsessed.

00:37:57.974 --> 00:38:03.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Plato wrote about
 Atlantis in 360BC, describing
 an advanced metropolis</i>

00:38:03.914 --> 00:38:09.854
<i> 9,000 years before his own
 time, lost beneath the waves
 in a cataclysmic event.</i>

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:19.062
<i> Marc Gutscher has analyzed
 Plato's texts, searching
 for clues to its location.</i>

00:38:19.429 --> 00:38:24.434
-'This power came forth out
of the Atlantic Ocean from an
island situated in front of

00:38:24.535 --> 00:38:29.039 align:start size:81% position:19%
the straits which
are by you called the
Pillars of Heracles.'

00:38:30.807 --> 00:38:35.679 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: The Pillars of
 Heracles is the name given by
 Plato to the maritime boundary</i>

00:38:35.779 --> 00:38:38.481
<i> of the ancient Greek world.</i>

00:38:38.815 --> 00:38:40.584 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> Many locations have
 been suggested.</i>

00:38:40.684 --> 00:38:45.656
<i> But Marc believes he
 has the answer: The
 Straits of Gibraltar.</i>

00:38:46.790 --> 00:38:52.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
-So here at Cape Spartel we're
at the northern edge of the
Moroccan Atlantic coast and

00:38:52.763 --> 00:38:55.499
just at the entrance to
the Straits of Gibraltar.

00:38:55.599 --> 00:39:00.103 align:start size:88% position:13%
About 10 to 15 kilometers
off to the north west
there's a submerged bank.

00:39:00.203 --> 00:39:03.674
Some people have suggested
that it might have been
inhabited and it might have

00:39:03.774 --> 00:39:07.044
formed the origin of
the Atlantis legend.

00:39:08.545 --> 00:39:14.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Today, this area
 sits under the second busiest
 shipping route in the world,</i>

00:39:14.284 --> 00:39:17.654
<i> a difficult place for
 sub-sea investigation.</i>

00:39:19.189 --> 00:39:23.828 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> During a project to study
 rising sea levels here,
 Marc gets his chance.</i>

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:32.269
<i> Using a remotely operated
 vehicle or ROV, he sets
 out to map the sea floor.</i>

00:39:38.175 --> 00:39:40.911
(waves crashing)

00:39:45.682 --> 00:39:47.818
<i> It's challenging work.</i>

00:39:48.986 --> 00:39:52.789
-It was very difficult,
both the towing of the
camera was difficult,

00:39:52.889 --> 00:39:57.060 align:start size:94% position:6%
keeping the ship in place
was difficult, and especially
trying to keep the ROV

00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:59.196 align:start size:81% position:19%
in place was really,
really difficult.

00:40:03.334 --> 00:40:07.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Finally, Marc
 gets the sonar scanning
 data that he needs.</i>

00:40:13.577 --> 00:40:17.948 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Now, combining
 this information with the
 latest computer graphics,</i>

00:40:18.048 --> 00:40:24.187
<i> it's possible to drain away
 the sea water from between the
 ancient pillars of Heracles,</i>

00:40:25.188 --> 00:40:31.162 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> and attempt to solve one of
 the greatest archaeological
 mysteries of all time...</i>

00:40:32.329 --> 00:40:35.466
<i> Soon, rocks begin
 to emerge, and the</i>

00:40:35.566 --> 00:40:40.370
<i> unmistakable outline of an
 island gradually appears.</i>

00:40:40.937 --> 00:40:46.644 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Is this the site of
 Atlantis, exposed for the
 first time in 11,000 years?</i>

00:40:49.112 --> 00:40:54.118
<i> Once again, important clues
 lie in Plato's own writing.</i>

00:40:54.218 --> 00:40:59.356
-Plato says that the city, and
all of its war-like men, were
destroyed in one day and night

00:41:01.358 --> 00:41:06.897 align:start size:84% position:16%
of terrible misfortune,
caused by strong
earthquakes and floods.

00:41:09.533 --> 00:41:14.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The island revealed
 by draining the ocean,
 sits on a subduction zone,</i>

00:41:14.972 --> 00:41:20.844 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> a boundary between the huge
 tectonic plates that make
 up the Earth's surface.</i>

00:41:20.944 --> 00:41:25.549
<i> The exact point where the
 biggest earthquakes strike.</i>

00:41:25.882 --> 00:41:31.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> A series of powerful
 earthquakes and tsunamis could
 have obliterated Atlantis and</i>

00:41:31.388 --> 00:41:36.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> sent the island
 on which it rests down
 into the ocean depths.</i>

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:41.398 align:start size:97% position:3%
-You could imagine that during
great earthquakes there might
have been some subsidence,

00:41:41.498 --> 00:41:47.103
so if we were to take into
account maybe 20 or 30 meters
of subsidence due to those

00:41:48.104 --> 00:41:52.743 align:start size:88% position:13%
earthquakes, there might
have been a somewhat
larger island there.

00:41:53.377 --> 00:41:57.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The geology
 supports Plato's account of
 the destruction of Atlantis.</i>

00:41:58.014 --> 00:42:02.052
<i> But one other vital piece
 of evidence is still needed.</i>

00:42:07.290 --> 00:42:10.560 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Draining
 the ocean has revealed
 a mysterious island</i>

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:14.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> exactly where Plato
 said Atlantis would be.</i>

00:42:14.331 --> 00:42:20.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But one other piece of
 evidence must be present for
 it actually to be Atlantis:</i>

00:42:20.337 --> 00:42:24.274 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The remains of substantial
 man-made structures.</i>

00:42:24.374 --> 00:42:28.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Using the drained landscape,
 it's possible to study
 the island's surface</i>

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:31.948 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> in extraordinary
 three-dimensional detail.</i>

00:42:32.849 --> 00:42:37.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Searching carefully for
 the remains of walls,
 temples, bridges,</i>

00:42:37.521 --> 00:42:40.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> and anything constructed
 by human hands.</i>

00:42:41.692 --> 00:42:43.426
<i> But there are none.</i>

00:42:44.027 --> 00:42:49.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Nowhere on this mysterious
 island are there any lines or
 geometric shapes which would</i>

00:42:49.199 --> 00:42:52.936
<i> indicate the presence of
 a great, ancient city.</i>

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:57.908
MARC: Atlantis did not exist
in the tiny island of Spartel.

00:42:58.308 --> 00:43:02.145 line:0%
If it did exist traces of
it have not been found.

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:08.818
<i> NARRATOR: However, the
 story of this submerged
 island is not over yet.</i>

00:43:08.918 --> 00:43:12.656
<i> There may be no trace of
 a city built out of stone.</i>

00:43:12.756 --> 00:43:18.028 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> But evidence found on
 land nearby suggests that
 during the Stone Age,</i>

00:43:18.128 --> 00:43:22.265
<i> when sea levels were much
 lower, people did live here</i>

00:43:22.365 --> 00:43:25.068
<i> and in many other
 places just like it.</i>

00:43:25.168 --> 00:43:29.940
<i> And during this period,
 our human ancestors make
 some of the most important</i>

00:43:30.040 --> 00:43:32.509
<i> breakthroughs of all time.</i>

00:43:32.842 --> 00:43:38.448
<i> They learn how to farm,
 keep domesticated animals,
 use sophisticated tools,</i>

00:43:38.549 --> 00:43:43.320
<i> build wooden houses
 and begin to trade.</i>

00:43:43.554 --> 00:43:47.858
<i> Then, the last Ice
 Age comes to an end.</i>

00:43:52.696 --> 00:43:59.036 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Over the course of
 15,000 years, sea
 levels rise by 400 feet.</i>

00:43:59.469 --> 00:44:02.906 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> And the territory of these
 early people vanishes.</i>

00:44:05.275 --> 00:44:09.546
<i> Their tools, homes and
 weapons are washed away.</i>

00:44:10.313 --> 00:44:16.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> That elemental human story
 may help explain the timeless
 appeal of the Atlantis legend.</i>

00:44:17.521 --> 00:44:21.892
PATRICK: There are many,
many stories out there in
the world that recall times

00:44:21.992 --> 00:44:25.796
when land that was once
inhabited was submerged.

00:44:25.896 --> 00:44:30.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
Did that information
inform Plato's fiction
about Atlantis?

00:44:31.267 --> 00:44:36.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
JON: I think there's no doubt,
that almost every culture
in the world has a flood myth,

00:44:36.339 --> 00:44:39.342 align:start size:91% position:9%
a deluge story, because
we developed, as a species,

00:44:39.442 --> 00:44:41.478
within this period
of rising sea level.

00:44:41.578 --> 00:44:46.216
So, that for me, is so
fundamental to understanding
ourselves as a species,

00:44:46.316 --> 00:44:48.085
that we should be
looking into this.

00:44:49.819 --> 00:44:53.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Plato's story of
 Atlantis is about human folly</i>

00:44:53.957 --> 00:44:57.661 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> and the destructive
 power of nature.</i>

00:44:58.862 --> 00:45:05.169
<i> Today, the rising level
 of the oceans threatens
 all coastal communities.</i>

00:45:06.503 --> 00:45:12.109 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Great port cities like
 Bangkok, Ho Chi Minh City
 and Dhaka are all at risk.</i>

00:45:21.184 --> 00:45:26.690
<i> And in North America there's
 one of particular concern.</i>

00:45:27.024 --> 00:45:29.058
<i> New Orleans.</i>

00:45:29.159 --> 00:45:33.930 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Sitting on the banks of the
 great Mississippi River,
 the city's average height</i>

00:45:34.030 --> 00:45:37.067
<i> is six feet below sea level.</i>

00:45:37.334 --> 00:45:40.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> This makes it highly
 vulnerable to flooding.</i>

00:45:41.938 --> 00:45:45.442
<i> In 2005, disaster strikes.</i>

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:51.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Hurricane Katrina
 causes a massive storm
 surge and the levees,</i>

00:45:51.815 --> 00:45:55.085 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> designed to protect
 the city, are breached.</i>

00:45:56.419 --> 00:45:59.690
<i> The resulting floods
 are catastrophic.</i>

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:05.328
<i> Nearly 2,000 people die and
 many more are left homeless.</i>

00:46:05.896 --> 00:46:09.432
<i> And the situation in
 New Orleans is getting
 worse, because the</i>

00:46:09.532 --> 00:46:12.569
<i> city itself is sinking.</i>

00:46:13.437 --> 00:46:17.507
<i> Due in part to the removal of
 groundwater, some districts of</i>

00:46:17.607 --> 00:46:21.278 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> New Orleans are subsiding
 by two inches a year.</i>

00:46:21.845 --> 00:46:24.648
JIM: Given ongoing
sea level rise, which
is a scientific fact,

00:46:24.748 --> 00:46:28.418 align:start size:91% position:9%
the time will come when
some of our settlements in
our time are under water.

00:46:28.518 --> 00:46:32.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
Cities like New Orleans,
they all have suffered
incursions of water.

00:46:32.522 --> 00:46:36.426
And the time will come when
we will no longer be able
to battle that incursion.

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:44.401 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: By the turn of the
 next century, New Orleans
 could be uninhabitable.</i>

00:46:49.072 --> 00:46:54.678 line:0%
<i> A mysterious underwater
 city, studied by
 maritime archaeologists.</i>

00:46:57.714 --> 00:46:59.917 line:0%
<i> A modern-day Atlantis.</i>

00:47:05.922 --> 00:47:07.124 line:0%
Captioned by Cotter
Captioning Services.

